    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d983">

                <pb facs="1477" n="1463" xml:id="pg1463"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 LCC/23: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the United
                        Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-05-01T15:00:00-04:00">May 1, 1944—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3484. For Bucknell, reference Embassy’s despatch No. 14, 940, April 8,
                        1944.<anchor n="18" xml:id="d983-f1"/> We have concluded that it would be
                    unwise to press the Soviet Government further with regard to membership on the
                    London Propaganda Coordinating Committee. In commenting upon Ambassador
                    Harriman’s several inquiries regarding the Soviet Government’s attitude towards
                    the parallel American and British invitations to join the Committee, the Soviet
                    authorities have indicated that they regard the situation as very complex,
                    requiring much study and fraught with many obstacles. We are of the opinion that
                    we have done as much as we reasonably should in extending the invitation. The
                    door is open to the Soviet Government whenever it wishes to avail itself of the
                    Committee’s facilities.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
